---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Launch"
  description="The Clientless launch page: backend-composed applications streamed through json-render to every host."
>
  <section class="page hero">
    <div>
      <h1>The app no longer has to live in the client.</h1>
      <p>
        Clientless moves composition to the backend. Product logic, data,
        tools, AI generation, and workflow state produce json-render streams.
        Hosts become small, standardized renderers.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button" href="/docs/thesis/">Read the thesis</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/blog/the-clientless-thesis/">Read the first essay</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-panel" aria-label="Clientless summary diagram">
      <div class="system-stack">
        <div class="node">
          <strong>Backend composition</strong>
          <span>Any language, any service shape, any tools, one UI contract.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stream">
          <div class="stream-line"></div>
          <span>json-render stream</span>
        </div>
        <div class="node host">
          <strong>Every host</strong>
          <span>OpenAI, Claude, WebHost, mobile, desktop, widgets, and more.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <h2>The new medium between frontend and backend is a stream.</h2>
    <p>
      Instead of shipping one large client application that owns routing,
      rendering, data fetching, and workflow composition, the backend emits a
      structured UI stream. The host renders that stream with the right
      registry for the surface.
    </p>
    <div class="diagram-panel">
      <div class="system-map">
        <div class="system-stack">
          <div class="node">
            <strong>Data and state</strong>
            <span>Databases, files, auth, sessions, queues, and domain models.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="node">
            <strong>Tools and AI</strong>
            <span>MCP tools, agents, model calls, search, and external services.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="node">
            <strong>Product composer</strong>
            <span>Monolith, services, workflows, or endpoint binaries.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stream">
          <div class="stream-line"></div>
          <span>SpecStream</span>
        </div>
        <div class="host-grid">
          <div class="node host">
            <strong>Native MCP hosts</strong>
            <span>Conversation and IDE hosts that render MCP Apps inline.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="node host">
            <strong>WebHost</strong>
            <span>A browser shell for non-native web delivery.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="node host alt">
            <strong>Mobile shells</strong>
            <span>iOS apps, widgets, and future React Native registries.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="node host alt">
            <strong>Desktop shells</strong>
            <span>macOS and Windows shells without app logic inside them.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <h2>The protocol stack becomes the product boundary.</h2>
    <p>
      Clientless does not eliminate frontend code. It changes where the product
      is composed. React, Swift, native widgets, and web components can still
      exist inside renderers and host shells, but the app contract is json.
    </p>
    <div class="diagram-panel">
      <div class="stack-visual">
        <div class="layer">
          <b>Surface</b>
          <span>Chat, browser, IDE, mobile widget, desktop panel, static site.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <b>Host adapter</b>
          <span>Native MCP Apps support, WebHost, or a thin platform shell.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <b>Renderer registry</b>
          <span>Maps the shared catalog to platform components and actions.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <b>json-render</b>
          <span>Specs, streams, state binding, validation, and progressive UI.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <b>MCP Apps</b>
          <span>Standard app resources, iframe bridge, and tool-call mediation.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <b>Backend</b>
          <span>Business logic, data, tools, AI, auth, and workflow composition.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <h2>One app model, many host categories.</h2>
    <p>
      Native MCP hosts can render the app directly. Non-native targets only
      need a shell that understands the same contract. The shell should be
      boring; the product stays upstream.
    </p>
    <div class="diagram-panel">
      <div class="platform-matrix">
        <div class="platform">
          <small>Native</small>
          <strong>OpenAI</strong>
          <span>ChatGPT Apps SDK is built on MCP and is a primary host target.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="platform">
          <small>Native</small>
          <strong>Claude</strong>
          <span>Claude and Claude Desktop are current MCP Apps targets.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="platform">
          <small>Native</small>
          <strong>Developer tools</strong>
          <span>VS Code GitHub Copilot, Goose, Postman, MCPJam, and Cursor-class clients.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="platform">
          <small>Web shell</small>
          <strong>WebHost</strong>
          <span>A browser adapter for static sites, SSR, and public web apps.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="platform">
          <small>Mobile shell</small>
          <strong>iOS</strong>
          <span>App screens and widgets backed by the same stream and catalog.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="platform">
          <small>Desktop shell</small>
          <strong>macOS</strong>
          <span>A native window or menu-bar host with no product logic inside.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="platform">
          <small>Desktop shell</small>
          <strong>Windows</strong>
          <span>A native host for task panes, dashboards, and assistant surfaces.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="platform">
          <small>Future</small>
          <strong>Endpoint binaries</strong>
          <span>Compile or package backend endpoints when a native binary is the right deployment unit.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <h2>Clientless is the Electron moment for AI-native apps, without the heavy shell.</h2>
    <p>
      Electron and Tauri gave developers a way to bring web apps to many
      platforms. Clientless aims higher: one backend-composed product surface,
      portable across native MCP hosts, the web, mobile shells, desktop shells,
      and static delivery.
    </p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Backend freedom</h3>
        <p>Use TypeScript, Python, Go, Rust, workflows, services, or a monolith.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Thin hosts</h3>
        <p>Hosts render, bridge actions, provide capability context, and stay small.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Shared product</h3>
        <p>The same composition can become a chat app, web app, widget, or desktop view.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

